; adder_model.tpu — the entire `adder_ternary_vanilla` model, one program.
;
; model/transformer.py's whole forward pass for the shipped ternary adder config
; (d=128, f=128, 4 layers, 4 heads, head_dim=32, T=32, vocab=13), in its
; *current* form: no bias, no positional encoding, DyT for normalization, ReLU
; attention, ReLU feed-forward, dropout off. Four transformer layers and the
; output head, in one run:
;
;     for L in 0..3:
;         Q,K,V = Wq(X), Wk(X), Wv(X)          # ternary weights, no bias
;         K,V   = trit(K), trit(V)             # ternary *activations*
;         S     = Q @ K^T / sqrt(head_dim)     # per head, on the MXU
;         P     = relu(S + causal_mask)        # NOT softmax
;         A     = P @ V                        # [T, 4*32] = [T, 128], on the MXU
;         X     = norm1(X + (Wo(A) + X))       # DyT; note the DOUBLE residual
;         X     = norm2(X + W2(relu(W1(X))))   # DyT
;     logits = X @ fc.weight                   # ternary head, on the MXU
;
; Nothing in this program runs on the VPU's matmul any more. `Model.fc` is a
; TernaryLinear too, so the output head is a `matmul_t` like everything else, and
; `vecmatmul` — which was the head's last caller after ternary K and V took
; attention away from it — is now unreachable from this kernel. The VPU is left
; with the elementwise passes and the narrows.
;
; Three things in that loop a reader of the class hierarchy would not predict,
; and that this kernel therefore has to reproduce deliberately:
;
;   * **K and V are ternary, Q and P are int8.** The MXU multiplies an int8
;     activation by a *ternary weight*, so the only way an attention matmul can
;     run on the array is if the operand that lands on the weight side is
;     ternary — K in `Q @ K^T`, V in `P @ V`. Both are, so both matmuls are
;     `matmul_t` dispatches. They used to be `vecmatmul`, which issues one
;     serial int8 dot product per output element and was the slowest thing in
;     the program; the VPU now runs only the elementwise passes and the head.
;     The narrow that produces a trit is `tquant`: `requant`'s fixed point
;     clipped to +-1 and written 2 bits wide in the array's own weight encoding,
;     so no repacking pass sits between the projection and the matmul.
;   * `MultiHeadAttention.forward` ends in `return O + X`, and
;     `Transformer.forward` then adds `X` again — so the attention residual is
;     `2X + O`, not `X + O`. `vecadd` takes two int8 operands, so that is two
;     adds and an extra narrow between them (RQ_XO, pinned to {1,0} because the
;     second add needs both sides back on X's scale).
;   * `norm1`/`norm2` are DyT — `hardtanh(alpha*x, -1, 1)` with one learned
;     scalar, no gamma/beta (arxiv 2503.10622). On this datapath that is not a
;     new pass at all: it is the narrow the residual add already needed, with
;     the multiplier carrying `alpha` and the output scale pinned to 1/127 so
;     the clip *is* the hardtanh. The `dyt` instruction is `requant` with a
;     symmetric +-127 clip, because hardtanh is odd and int8's -128 is not.
;     ../adder_kernel.md §4.
;
; The host supplies the embedded X (there is no positional encoding to add any
; more), both layers' weights, the mask, the requant words and the quantized
; `fc` weight; it gets the int32 logits back, plus every layer's X as a
; checkpoint. Embedding and argmax stay on the host: the ISA has no gather, and
; `redmax` returns the max rather than its index.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Why this is one program
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The four layers' ternary weights are 96 KB packed, and the last of them starts
; at 0x1AC00 — far past the 64 KB line. That used to be unreachable:
; `scalar_unit.sv` truncated a rdmem/wrmem DRAM address to ADDR_W = 16 bits, so
; a program could only name the low 64 KB of the 512 KB SRAM chip, even though
; the DMA, the SRAM controller and the UART host all addressed the full 19 bits.
; The scalar unit now emits MEM_ADDR_W, so the whole chip is program-visible and
; the model fits with 432 KB to spare.
;
; Two consequences shape the code below:
;
;   * X never round-trips through DRAM between layers — it lives in the
;     scratchpad and each layer updates it in place. The per-layer `wrmem` near
;     the bottom is a *checkpoint* for verification, not a data path.
;   * Weights are addressed by a running base register `lay_base`, advanced one
;     layer block per iteration. `li` takes a 16-bit immediate and a layer block
;     base leaves that range as soon as the model has more than two layers
;     (layer 3's block starts at 0x1AC00), so the base is built by `adds` — which
;     is exactly why the globals sit below 0x5000, where they *are* li-able.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The four sharp edges, stated once
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 1. `cfg vlen` is 10 bits, so the maximum VPU vector length is 1023 — and a
;    [32][32] score block is exactly 1024 elements. `vlen = 1024` reads as 0 and
;    the dispatch silently does nothing. Every elementwise pass here therefore
;    runs in CHUNK = 512-element pieces.
;
; 2. `vecadd` adds two int8 *operands*, so a residual add only means anything if
;    both sides are at the same scale. That is a constraint on the host's
;    requant words, not on this program: RQ_O and RQ_XO must land on X's scale
;    and RQ_F must land W2's output on X1's. ../adder_kernel.md §4.
;
; 3. DyT is what bounds the residual stream, and it is the reason this kernel is
;    worth running at int8 at all. Without normalization nothing renormalizes X
;    between layers, each layer's requant multiplies it by a constant, and the
;    error compounds geometrically — which is the 0.00% exact-sequence result in
;    ../adder_kernel.md §7.6. `norm2` bounds its output to [-1, 1]
;    *analytically*, so every layer after the first enters at a known scale of
;    1/127 with no calibration involved.
;
; 4. `tquant` writes 2 bits per element, i.e. one byte per four, so its `vlen`
;    must be a multiple of 4 and its destination pointer advances at a *quarter*
;    the rate of its source. CHUNK/4 = PKCH = 128 is that step. Both packed
;    tensors are exactly 1024 bytes, which is also why nothing has to be
;    rebuilt per head: a head is a byte offset into the packed block, because
;    the array reads a weight column as a contiguous run of trits.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Reading this file
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Six conventions carry most of the program. They are stated once here rather
; than repeated at each of the places they apply.
;
; * **Destination first, always.** `matmul_t.rq dst, act, wgt`,
;   `vecadd dst, src0, src1`, `requant dst, src, rq_word`,
;   `adds dst, a, b`. The two exceptions are the DMA pair, where the operand
;   order is *side*, not direction: `rdmem scratch, dram` fills the scratchpad
;   and `wrmem scratch, dram` spills to DRAM, and both name the scratchpad
;   first. The direction is in the mnemonic, not the operand order.
;
; * **Names are addresses.** No register in this program holds a value the
;   model cares about; they hold byte addresses, six loop strides and two
;   counters. Tensors never pass through the register file — a dispatch reads
;   its operands' *contents* as addresses and streams the data itself.
;
; * **`setcfg` is sticky, and it is half of every dispatch's meaning.** An
;   instruction word names three registers and an opcode; the shape it operates
;   on comes from config registers that persist until overwritten. `matmul_t`
;   reads arow/wcol/crow/ktiles/ntiles/tlen/scalar; the VPU ops read vlen; the
;   DMA reads len (and tcols/tsrow/tdrow when transposing). So a `setcfg` block
;   applies to every following dispatch, not just the next one. There are five
;   MXU-geometry blocks below — §1, twice inside §4, again at the top of §5, and
;   §8 — and they are exactly the five places the matmul shape changes. §6 has
;   none, because the feed-forward's shape is §5's (F == D).
;
; * **Dispatch is issue-and-wait.** The scalar unit stalls until the unit it
;   started raises `done`, so there is no `wait` instruction anywhere below and
;   no read-after-write hazard to reason about: when an instruction retires its
;   result is in the scratchpad. It also means overwriting a dispatch's own
;   input is safe — see `xo_add_loop`, which writes O over itself.
;
; * **The int32 sandwich.** The VPU reads int8 and writes int32; only
;   `requant`, `dyt` and `tquant` go the other way. So every elementwise step
;   is two dispatches — compute into `acc_i32`, narrow back out — and a lone
;   `vecadd` or `relu` would leave the result in a width nothing downstream can
;   read.
;
; * **One loop idiom, used eight times.** Every counted loop is:
;
;       li      loop_i, 0            ; ...and pointers, and `loop_n` = bound
;   label:
;       <body>
;       adds    <ptr>, <ptr>, <step> ; one `adds` per pointer that walks
;       adds    loop_i, loop_i, one
;       cmps    loop_i, loop_n
;       blt     label
;
;   `cmps` sets the flags and `blt` consumes them, so nothing may sit between
;   them. Six of the eight walk CHUNKs of a [T][D] activation (NCHUNK = 8
;   iterations), one walks the four heads (`head_loop`), and the last is the
;   layer loop itself, which uses `layer_i` in place of `loop_i` because
;   `loop_i` belongs to whatever inner loop ran most recently.
;
;     python ../assembler.py adder_model.tpu --listing
;     python ../gen_vectors.py -p examples/adder_model.tpu

; ===========================================================================
; Geometry — model/transformer.py adder_ternary_vanilla()
; ===========================================================================
; **These `.equ` names are a host ABI, not private labels.** The assembler hands
; its whole constant table back to the caller, and `gen_vectors.py`,
; `adder_export.py`, `torch_ref.py` and `host/run_adder.py` all index it *by
; name* — T, D, D3, VOCAB, VPAD, WCOL, LAYERS, LSTRIDE, D_L0, D_XIN, D_MASK,
; D_WFC, D_LOG and every O_* offset. `gen_vectors.program_kind` even identifies
; which program it is holding by looking for `LSTRIDE`. Renaming one is an API
; break that the assembler cannot catch and that shows up as a staging bug at
; the far end; the `.reg` aliases and the labels, by contrast, are local to this
; file and safe to rename.
;
; The comments give each constant's resolved value where it is not obvious;
; anything written as an expression is evaluated at assembly time, so nothing
; below costs an instruction.
.equ T        32            ; tokens (train.py --max_tokens; <= 63 for cfg tlen)
.equ D        128           ; model dim
.equ HEADS    4             ; q_heads == kv_heads, so heads_per_q = 1
.equ DH       32            ; head_dim = D // HEADS
.equ D3       D * 3         ; fused Q|K|V weight block: 3D columns
.equ F        128           ; feed-forward hidden dim
.equ VOCAB    13            ; len(numbers_data.VOCAB)
; Depth is the only thing that moves when the model gets bigger: `gen_vectors.py`,
; `torch_ref.py` and `host/run_adder.py` all read LAYERS out of this table, and the
; per-layer body below is a loop over `lay_base`, not unrolled. It went 4 -> 2
; when the QAT retrain fitted at two layers, and back to 4 when ternary K/V and a
; ternary head cost enough accuracy to need the depth back.
.equ LAYERS   4

.equ ROWS     8             ; MXU array contraction dim
.equ COLS     8             ; MXU array output cols

; The array writes whole COLS-wide output tiles, so a 13-column head would have
; its last tile spill three int32 words past the end of each logit row and land
; on the *next* row's first three — the output-tile index is the outer loop, so
; it would corrupt every row but the last. Rounding the head's output up to a
; whole tile is the fix: 16 columns, of which the host stages the top three as
; zero trits and reads 13 of every 16 back. See section 8.
.equ VPAD     (VOCAB + COLS - 1) // COLS * COLS  ; head output cols, padded = 16

; Tile counts, one pair per matmul shape in the program. The three names that
; contract over D are the projections and the feed-forward; the four short ones
; are attention, where the contraction is a head_dim (scores) or the token axis
; (values). DH and T are both 32 today, so KTDH/NTT/KTT/NTDH all come out at 4 —
; they are still written separately, because a change to either would move only
; two of the four.
.equ KTD      D // ROWS     ; contraction tiles, D-deep matmul       = 16
.equ NTD      D // COLS     ; output tiles, 128 columns              = 16
.equ NTV      VPAD // COLS  ; output tiles, the padded head          =  2
.equ KTDH     DH // ROWS    ; contraction tiles, Q @ K^T             =  4
.equ NTT      T // COLS     ; output tiles, T keys                   =  4
.equ KTT      T // ROWS     ; contraction tiles, P @ V               =  4
.equ NTDH     DH // COLS    ; output tiles, one head's DH columns    =  4

.equ CHUNK    512           ; VPU pass length (cfg vlen is 10 bits: max 1023)
.equ PKCH     CHUNK // 4    ; ...and the bytes one `tquant` pass writes   = 128
.equ NCHUNK   T * D // CHUNK    ; passes over a [T][D] activation = 8

; ---- strides (bytes) ------------------------------------------------------
.equ AROW     D             ; activation row stride: D int8             = 128
.equ WCOL     D * 2 // 8    ; ternary column stride, D trits deep       = 32
.equ TCOL     T * 2 // 8    ; ternary column stride, T trits deep       =  8
.equ KHOFF    DH * 2 // 8   ; head step inside a packed K row           =  8
.equ VHOFF    DH * TCOL     ; head step inside packed V^T             = 256
.equ CROWD    D * 4         ; int32 row stride of a [T][D] out          = 512
.equ SROW     T * 4         ; int32 row stride of a [T][T] block        = 128
.equ LROW     VPAD * 4      ; int32 row stride of the [T][VPAD] logits  = 64

; ===========================================================================
; Scratchpad map (64 KB; highest byte used is 0xCC3F)
; ===========================================================================
; The last column names the register that permanently points at each buffer, so
; a register name below can be read straight back to a shape. Four buffers have
; no permanent register — WBUF's contents change five times a layer, and T1/T2
; change role three times — and are carried by `buf_a`/`buf_b` at each use.
;
;                                                                  held by
.equ WBUF     0x0000        ; weight window, refilled 4x/layer     (12288 B)  wgt_win
.equ Q8       0x3000        ; [T][D] int8 Q                         (4096 B)  q_head+h*DH
.equ K8       0x4000        ; [T][D] int8 K, before ternarizing     (4096 B)  (buf_a)
.equ V8       0x5000        ; [T][D] int8 V, before transposing     (4096 B)  (buf_a)
.equ VT       0x6000        ; [D][T] int8, V transposed, all heads   (4096 B)  vt_i8
.equ X        0x7000        ; [T][D] int8 residual stream, in place  (4096 B)  x_res
.equ T1       0x8000        ; [T][D] int8 temp: A, H, F              (4096 B)  a_head, buf_*
.equ T2       0x9000        ; [T][D] int8 temp: O, relu(H)           (4096 B)  (buf_b)
.equ S32      0xA000        ; [T][VPAD] int32 logits                 (2048 B)  log_i32
.equ TMP32    0xB000        ; CHUNK int32 elementwise temp           (2048 B)  acc_i32
.equ S8       0xB800        ; [T][T] int8 scores                     (1024 B)  scr_lo/scr_hi
.equ P8       0xBC00        ; [T][T] int8 attention weights          (1024 B)  att_lo/att_hi
.equ KTP      0xC000        ; [T][D] trits — K as an MXU weight      (1024 B)  kt_head+h*KHOFF
.equ VTP      0xC400        ; [D][T] trits — V^T as an MXU weight    (1024 B)  vt_head+h*VHOFF
.equ MASK     0xC800        ; [T][T] int8 causal mask, 0 / -128      (1024 B)  msk_lo/msk_hi
.equ RQW      0xCC00        ; this layer's 16 requant {m0,n} words      (64 B)  RQ_* immediates
.equ LOG      S32           ; the logits reuse the score-era buffer            log_i32

; T1 and T2 are the only buffers whose meaning changes within a layer, and the
; sequence is worth having in one place, because `buf_a`/`buf_b` do not say it:
;
;   T1:  A (section 4 writes it per head)  ->  H (§6)  ->  F (§6)
;   T2:  O (§5)  ->  XO, written over O (§5)  ->  relu(H) (§6)
;
; Nothing else aliases: Q8/K8/V8 are live only until K and V are packed, and
; S8/P8 only within one head.

; The two packed blocks are what the MXU reads as weight columns, and their
; layout is not a choice — it is the array's, reached by writing trits in the
; order they already lie in:
;
;   KTP  row t is K[t][:] packed, WCOL = 32 bytes. Column `s` of K^T is row `s`
;        of K, so a *row-major* pack is already column-major for the transpose,
;        and head h is the byte offset KHOFF*h into every row. That is why Q@K^T
;        needs no transpose at all.
;   VTP  row d is V^T[d][:] = V[:,d] packed, TCOL = 8 bytes. P@V contracts over
;        keys, which is V's *row* axis, so this one does need the transpose —
;        and the DMA can only transpose bytes, which is why V passes through
;        int8 (V8 -> D_VT -> VT) before it is ternarized.

; ---- the requant words, in the order adder_kernel.md §4 lists them ---------
; Reloaded from the layer block each iteration, so these addresses are fixed
; and every `setcfg scalar` stays a compile-time immediate. Four of the sixteen
; are consumed by an op other than `requant` — two `dyt` and two `tquant` — but
; the word format is identical, so DRAM staging does not care which.
.equ RQ_Q     RQW +  0      ; X @ Wq   -> Q      int8
.equ RQ_K     RQW +  4      ; X @ Wk   -> K      int8
.equ RQ_KT    RQW +  8      ; K        -> trits  `tquant`
.equ RQ_V     RQW + 12      ; X @ Wv   -> V      int8
.equ RQ_VT    RQW + 16      ; V^T      -> trits  `tquant`
.equ RQ_S     RQW + 20      ; Q@K^T int32 -> S8  (folds 1/sqrt(head_dim))
.equ RQ_ID    RQW + 24      ; {m0=1, n=0} — narrow + clip only
.equ RQ_P     RQW + 28      ; relu(S8) -> P8     ({1,0} is the natural choice)
.equ RQ_A     RQW + 32      ; P@V int32 -> A8
.equ RQ_O     RQW + 36      ; A8 @ Wo  -> O      **must land on X's scale**
.equ RQ_XO    RQW + 40      ; X + O    -> XO     **{1,0}: still on X's scale**
.equ RQ_X1    RQW + 44      ; XO + X   -> X1     `dyt`: carries norm1's alpha
.equ RQ_H     RQW + 48      ; X1 @ W1  -> H
.equ RQ_HR    RQW + 52      ; relu(H)  -> HR     ({1,0} is the natural choice)
.equ RQ_F     RQW + 56      ; HR @ W2  -> F      **must land on X1's scale**
.equ RQ_X2    RQW + 60      ; X1 + F   -> X2     `dyt`: carries norm2's alpha

; ===========================================================================
; DRAM map (512 KB available; highest byte used is 0x21FFF = 136 KB)
; ===========================================================================
; Globals live below 0x5000 so every one of them fits a 16-bit `li`. The layer
; blocks sit above and are reached through the `lay_base` register instead.
.equ D_XIN    0x0000        ; [T][D] int8, the embedded input       (4096 B)
.equ D_MASK   0x1000        ; [T][T] int8 causal mask               (1024 B)
.equ D_A8     0x1400        ; [T][D] int8 attention out (scratch)   (4096 B)
.equ D_VT     0x2400        ; [D][T] int8 V^T (scratch)             (4096 B)
.equ D_WFC    0x3400        ; [D][VPAD] trits, fc.weight             (512 B)
.equ D_LOG    0x3C00        ; [T][VPAD] int32 logits — **the result** (2048 B)

.equ D_L0     0x5000        ; layer 0's block
.equ LSTRIDE  0x7400        ; per-layer block stride (29696 B)

; ---- offsets inside one layer block ---------------------------------------
.equ O_WQKV   0x0000        ; [D][3D] trits, col-major 2b          (12288 B)
.equ O_WO     0x3000        ; [D][D]  trits                         (4096 B)
.equ O_W1     0x4000        ; [D][F]  trits                         (4096 B)
.equ O_W2     0x5000        ; [F][D]  trits                         (4096 B)
.equ O_RQW    0x6000        ; this layer's 16 requant words            (64 B)
.equ O_XOUT   0x6400        ; [T][D] int8 — this layer's X, a checkpoint

; ---- byte counts ----------------------------------------------------------
.equ B_WQKV   D3 * WCOL     ; 12288
.equ B_WD     D * WCOL      ;  4096
.equ B_ACT    T * D         ;  4096   a [T][D] int8 activation
.equ B_VT     D * T         ;  4096
.equ B_SQ     T * T         ;  1024   a [T][T] int8 block
.equ B_TRIT   T * D * 2 // 8   ; 1024   a [T][D] tensor as packed trits
.equ B_RQW    64
.equ B_WFC    VPAD * WCOL   ;   512   the head is ternary: 32 bytes a column
.equ B_LOG    T * VPAD * 4  ;  2048

; ===========================================================================
; Registers — 30 of the 31 writable ones are live (r0 is a hardwired zero).
; ===========================================================================
; The scalar register file is the only place an address can live: every compute
; op reads the *contents* of the registers it names to get the scratchpad or
; DRAM byte address it hands to its unit. So an address is `li`'d into a
; register first, and a register name is best read as "what this address points
; at", not "what this value is".
;
; Four lifetimes, and the table is grouped by them — which one a register is in
; tells you whether clobbering it is safe:
;
;   whole-program   set once in section 0 and never written again. Every fixed
;                   scratchpad base and every loop stride is here, which is why
;                   the inner loops contain no `li` at all.
;   per-layer       rewritten at the top of each `layer_loop` iteration.
;   per-head        rewritten at the bottom of each `head_loop` iteration.
;   scratch         no invariant meaning; whoever writes it owns it until the
;                   next writer. Read the section, not the name.

; ---- scratch: rewritten constantly, meaning is local to the section --------
.reg blk_off  r1    ; byte offset *inside* a layer's DRAM block (O_WQKV, O_WO,
                    ; O_W1, O_W2, O_RQW, O_XOUT). It only ever exists to be
                    ; added to `lay_base` on the next line: a layer block base
                    ; is too large to `li`, but every offset into one is small.
.reg dram_p   r2    ; the DRAM byte address a `rdmem`/`wrmem` moves to or from.
                    ; Always the *second* operand of those two instructions;
                    ; the first is the scratchpad side, whichever way the data
                    ; is going.
.reg rq_word  r3    ; scratchpad address of the {m0,n} requant word the *VPU*
                    ; is about to read — the third operand of `requant`, `dyt`
                    ; and `tquant`. The MXU's own store-side requant does not
                    ; use this register: it reads `cfg scalar`. That is why a
                    ; matmul is preceded by `setcfg scalar, RQ_*` and a VPU
                    ; pass by `li rq_word, RQ_*`. Same 16 words, two paths.

; ---- whole-program: fixed scratchpad bases, all set in section 0 -----------
.reg wgt_win  r4    ; WBUF  — the weight window. Refilled from DRAM four times
                    ; a layer (fused QKV, Wo, W1, W2) and once more for the
                    ; output head. Only 12 KB of scratchpad because no two of
                    ; those five are ever needed at the same time.
.reg x_res    r5    ; X     — [T][D] int8 residual stream, and THE long-lived
                    ; tensor of the program: filled once before layer 0, then
                    ; updated in place by both `dyt` passes of every layer.
                    ; Never round-trips through DRAM.
.reg vt_i8    r6    ; VT    — [D][T] int8 V^T, the *source* the V ternary pack
                    ; reads (VTP is its destination). Still int8 at this point
                    ; because the transposing DMA that produced it moves bytes,
                    ; so V has to be transposed before it can be ternarized.
.reg log_i32  r7    ; S32   — [T][VPAD] int32. A scores-era name; today the
                    ; buffer holds only the final logits (`.equ LOG S32`), and
                    ; only after the layer loop has finished.
.reg acc_i32  r8    ; TMP32 — the int32 landing pad every VPU op writes to.
                    ; `vecadd` and `relu` read int8 and widen to int32, and
                    ; only `requant`/`dyt`/`tquant` narrow back, so every
                    ; elementwise step below is a *pair* of dispatches with
                    ; this buffer in the middle.
.reg scr_lo   r9    ; S8           — [T][T] int8 scores, first  CHUNK elements
.reg scr_hi   r10   ; S8 + CHUNK   — ...and the second 512.
.reg att_lo   r11   ; P8           — [T][T] int8 attention weights, first CHUNK
.reg att_hi   r12   ; P8 + CHUNK   — ...and the second.
.reg msk_lo   r13   ; MASK         — [T][T] int8 causal mask, first  CHUNK
.reg msk_hi   r14   ; MASK + CHUNK — ...and the second. Filled once for the
                    ; whole model: the mask does not depend on the layer.
                    ;
                    ; Those six exist as *pairs* because of sharp edge 1 — a
                    ; [32][32] block is 1024 elements and `cfg vlen` is 10 bits,
                    ; so it cannot be one pass. Two halves, two permanent
                    ; pointers, and the attention body stays straight-line
                    ; rather than becoming another counted loop.

; ---- whole-program: the loop strides, so no inner loop needs an `li` -------
.reg one      r23   ; the constant 1. It needs a register because `adds` is
                    ; register-register — there is no add-immediate — and every
                    ; loop below increments its counter with it.
.reg chunk_b  r24   ; CHUNK = 512 — one VPU pass of int8, in bytes. The step
                    ; for any pointer walking a [T][D] activation.
.reg pack_b   r25   ; PKCH  = 128 — what one `tquant` pass *writes*, in bytes:
                    ; CHUNK/4, because a trit is 2 bits (sharp edge 4). A loop
                    ; that ternarizes therefore steps its source by `chunk_b`
                    ; and its destination by `pack_b`, and the asymmetry is the
                    ; thing to check first if a packed block looks shifted.
.reg head_i8  r26   ; DH    =  32 — one head's column block inside an int8
                    ; [T][D] tensor, in bytes. Steps Q and A between heads.
.reg head_kt  r27   ; KHOFF =   8 — the same head step inside a *packed* K row:
                    ; DH trits = 8 bytes, an offset *within* every row.
.reg head_vt  r28   ; VHOFF = 256 — the head step inside packed V^T, which is
                    ; whole rows (DH rows of TCOL bytes each), not a within-row
                    ; offset. `head_kt` and `head_vt` differ because K is packed
                    ; row-major over D and V^T row-major over T.

; ---- per-layer ------------------------------------------------------------
.reg layer_i  r29   ; 0..LAYERS-1, the layer loop counter.
.reg lay_base r30   ; this layer's DRAM block base = D_L0 + layer_i*LSTRIDE.
                    ; Advanced by `adds` at the bottom of the layer and never
                    ; recomputed: layer 3's block starts at 0x1AC00, well past
                    ; what a 16-bit `li` can name (see "Why this is one
                    ; program"). Every weight read in a layer is therefore
                    ; `lay_base + blk_off`, never a bare immediate.

; ---- per-head (all four advanced at the bottom of `head_loop`) -------------
.reg q_head   r16   ; head h's Q columns:        Q8  + h*DH     (int8)
.reg kt_head  r17   ; head h's packed K columns: KTP + h*KHOFF  (trits)
.reg vt_head  r18   ; head h's packed V^T rows:  VTP + h*VHOFF  (trits)
.reg a_head   r15   ; where head h's output lands inside A:      T1  + h*DH.
                    ; The matmul writes straight into A's column block, so
                    ; there is no per-head gather or scatter step at all.

; ---- loop control, and the general-purpose pointer pair -------------------
.reg loop_i   r19   ; the inner loop counter — heads in section 4, CHUNKs
                    ; everywhere else.
.reg loop_n   r20   ; that loop's bound. Reused as a plain stride scratch in
                    ; section 7, once the inner loops are provably done.
.reg buf_a    r21   ; the general-purpose scratchpad-buffer pointers. T1 and T2
.reg buf_b    r22   ; have no permanent register — they are re-`li`'d at each
                    ; use — so these two carry whichever buffers the current
                    ; section is working on, and then double as the chunk-loop
                    ; pointers that walk them. Which of the two is the source
                    ; and which the destination is *not* fixed: in a matmul
                    ; `buf_a` is usually the destination, in a `tquant` loop it
                    ; is the source. Every use below names its buffer in a
                    ; comment for exactly that reason.

; ===========================================================================
; 0. Constants, fixed scratchpad bases, and the model-wide inputs
; ===========================================================================
    li          one,      1
    li          chunk_b,  CHUNK
    li          pack_b,   PKCH
    li          head_i8,  DH
    li          head_kt,  KHOFF
    li          head_vt,  VHOFF

    li          wgt_win,  WBUF
    li          x_res,    X
    li          vt_i8,    VT
    li          log_i32,  S32
    li          acc_i32,  TMP32
    li          scr_lo,   S8
    li          scr_hi,   S8 + CHUNK
    li          att_lo,   P8
    li          att_hi,   P8 + CHUNK
    li          msk_lo,   MASK
    li          msk_hi,   MASK + CHUNK

    ; X and the mask are loaded once for the whole model. X then stays resident
    ; and every layer updates it in place — no inter-layer DRAM round trip.
    li          dram_p,   D_XIN                   ; DRAM side
    setcfg      len,      B_ACT                   ; = T*D  = 4096 bytes
    rdmem       x_res,    dram_p                  ; DRAM D_XIN -> scratch X
    li          dram_p,   D_MASK
    setcfg      len,      B_SQ                    ; = T*T  = 1024 bytes
    rdmem       msk_lo,   dram_p                  ; DRAM D_MASK -> scratch MASK.
                                                  ; One fill covers both halves:
                                                  ; `msk_hi` just points into it.

    ; The one MXU config that is the same for every matmul in the program. The
    ; rest (arow/wcol/crow/ktiles/ntiles) now differ between the D-deep
    ; projections and the two attention matmuls, so they are set per shape.
    setcfg      tlen,     T                       ; 32 token rows, one MXU pass (TOK_W = 6 bits)

    li          lay_base, D_L0
    li          layer_i,  0

; ===========================================================================
; ============================ PER-LAYER BODY ===============================
; ===========================================================================
layer_loop:
    ; ---- this layer's requant words, into the fixed RQW slot -------------
    ; Reloading them per layer is what keeps every `setcfg scalar` below a
    ; compile-time immediate instead of needing `setcfgr`.
    li          blk_off,  O_RQW                   ; offset within the layer block
    adds        dram_p,   lay_base, blk_off       ; ...absolute DRAM address
    li          buf_a,    RQW                     ; the fixed scratchpad slot
    setcfg      len,      B_RQW                   ; = 64 bytes = 16 words
    rdmem       buf_a,    dram_p                  ; -> RQW; RQ_* are offsets into it

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 1. Q, K, V — three ternary matmuls out of one fused weight block
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; [Wq|Wk|Wv] is one [D][3D] block in DRAM, so it is one `rdmem`; but the three
; are *separate* matmuls because each carries its own absmean, hence its own
; requant word. Fusing them into a single 48-tile `matmul_t` would force one
; multiplier on all three (adder_kernel.md §4). Three 16-tile dispatches cost
; six extra instructions and keep each projection's scale its own.
;
; Unlike the previous revision the three land in three separate [T][D] buffers
; rather than interleaved into one [T][3D] block. `tquant` reads a contiguous
; vector, and K and V are both about to be ternarized, so a fused output would
; have to be de-interleaved first — the column stride that used to be free is
; now the thing in the way.

    adds        dram_p,   lay_base, r0            ; + O_WQKV, which is 0 — r0 is the
                                                  ; hardwired zero, so this is a move
    setcfg      len,      B_WQKV                  ; = D3*WCOL = 12288 bytes
    rdmem       wgt_win,  dram_p                  ; all three weight matrices at once

    ; The D-deep projection geometry. Five sticky config registers; every
    ; `matmul_t` until section 4 re-sets them reads exactly these.
    setcfg      arow,     AROW                    ; = 128: activation row stride (bytes)
    setcfg      wcol,     WCOL                    ; =  32: D trits, one weight column
    setcfg      ktiles,   KTD                     ; =  16: contraction tiles, D/ROWS
    setcfg      ntiles,   NTD                     ; =  16: output tiles, D/COLS
    setcfg      crow,     CROWD                   ; = 512: int32 result row stride. With
                                                  ; `.rq` the store is int8, so the row
                                                  ; is CROWD/4 = D bytes.

    ; matmul_t.rq  dst, activation, weight — `.rq` narrows int32 -> int8 on the
    ; store using the {m0,n} word at `cfg scalar`. Three dispatches over one
    ; loaded block: only the weight pointer and the requant word change.
    li          buf_a,    Q8                      ; dst
    setcfg      scalar,   RQ_Q
    matmul_t.rq buf_a,    x_res,    wgt_win       ; Q = X @ Wq   [T][D] int8

    li          buf_a,    K8                      ; dst
    li          buf_b,    WBUF + D * WCOL         ; ...Wk: columns 128..255
    setcfg      scalar,   RQ_K
    matmul_t.rq buf_a,    x_res,    buf_b         ; K = X @ Wk   [T][D] int8

    li          buf_a,    V8                      ; dst
    li          buf_b,    WBUF + 2 * D * WCOL     ; ...Wv: columns 256..383
    setcfg      scalar,   RQ_V
    matmul_t.rq buf_a,    x_res,    buf_b         ; V = X @ Wv   [T][D] int8

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 2. K -> trits, in place over the row axis
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; `tquant` narrows int8 -> {-1,0,1} and writes the trit in the array's 2-bit
; weight encoding, four elements to a byte. Packed row-major, K's row t becomes
; the WCOL-byte weight column `t` of K^T — so the transpose in Q@K^T is free,
; exactly as it was when this was a vecmatmul, but now the array does the
; arithmetic. Eight CHUNK passes cover [T][D]; the destination advances PKCH.

    setcfg      vlen,     CHUNK                   ; = 512 elements read per pass
    li          rq_word,  RQ_KT                   ; the VPU reads {m0,n} from here
    li          buf_a,    K8                      ; source: int8 K
    li          buf_b,    KTP                     ; destination: packed trits
    li          loop_i,   0
    li          loop_n,   NCHUNK                  ; = 8 passes over [T][D]
pack_k_loop:
    tquant      buf_b,    buf_a,    rq_word       ; dst, src, {m0,n}
    adds        buf_a,    buf_a,    chunk_b       ; source: +512 bytes...
    adds        buf_b,    buf_b,    pack_b        ; ...destination: +128. Sharp edge 4.
    adds        loop_i,   loop_i,   one
    cmps        loop_i,   loop_n
    blt         pack_k_loop

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 3. V^T — every head, in one transposing DMA — then V^T -> trits
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; P @ V contracts over *keys*, which is V's row axis, so the array's weight
; column is a column of V. The `.t` DMA is the only thing here that can reorder
; bytes, and it moves bytes, not trits — which is why V is ternarized *after*
; the transpose and not before. Head h's rows land at VT + h*DH*T, so the packed
; per-head base is the plain pointer step VHOFF.

    li          buf_a,    V8                      ; scratchpad side (the source here)
    li          dram_p,   D_VT                    ; DRAM side (the destination here)
    setcfg      tcols,    D                       ; D elements per source row...
    setcfg      tsrow,    AROW                    ; ...from rows D bytes apart
    setcfg      tdrow,    T                       ; destination rows are T tokens long
    setcfg      len,      B_VT                    ; = D*T = 4096 bytes
    wrmem.t     buf_a,    dram_p                  ; scratch V -> DRAM V^T. `.t` transposes;
                                                  ; the DMA is the only unit that can.
    rdmem       vt_i8,    dram_p                  ; DRAM V^T -> scratch VT, untransposed —
                                                  ; the mode is in the mnemonic, not in the
                                                  ; still-set tcols/tsrow/tdrow.

    setcfg      vlen,     CHUNK
    li          rq_word,  RQ_VT
    li          buf_a,    VT                      ; source: int8 V^T
    li          buf_b,    VTP                     ; destination: packed trits
    li          loop_i,   0
    li          loop_n,   NCHUNK
pack_v_loop:
    tquant      buf_b,    buf_a,    rq_word       ; dst, src, {m0,n}
    adds        buf_a,    buf_a,    chunk_b       ; +512 read...
    adds        buf_b,    buf_b,    pack_b        ; ...+128 written
    adds        loop_i,   loop_i,   one
    cmps        loop_i,   loop_n
    blt         pack_v_loop

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 4. Attention, one head at a time — both matmuls on the MXU
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Head h is a contiguous DH-column slice of Q (kv_heads == q_heads, so
; heads_per_q = 1 and the model's 5-D layout collapses to [T, 4, 32]) and a
; byte offset into each packed block. No reshape anywhere.

    ; Four pointers into four tensors, all at head 0, each advanced by its own
    ; stride at the bottom of the loop. The strides differ (head_i8/head_kt/
    ; head_vt) only because the four tensors are packed differently — the head
    ; index itself is the same h in all four.
    li          q_head,   Q8                      ; head 0's Q columns    (step head_i8)
    li          kt_head,  KTP                     ; head 0's packed K     (step head_kt)
    li          vt_head,  VTP                     ; head 0's packed V^T   (step head_vt)
    li          a_head,   T1                      ; head 0's A columns    (step head_i8)
    li          loop_i,   0
    li          loop_n,   HEADS                   ; = 4

head_loop:
    ; ---- S8 = requant(Q_h @ K_h^T) --------------------------------------
    ; K^T is never materialized: the packed weight column `s` *is* K's row `s`.
    ; The requant is the array's own store-side one, so RQ_S (which carries
    ; 1/sqrt(head_dim)) costs no separate pass — the two VPU requants the old
    ; vecmatmul needed to narrow its int32 block are gone with it.
    setcfg      arow,     AROW                    ; = 128: Q rows are D bytes apart
    setcfg      wcol,     WCOL                    ; =  32: a packed K row is D trits
    setcfg      crow,     SROW                    ; = 128: -> int8 rows of SROW/4 = T
    setcfg      ktiles,   KTDH                    ; =   4: contract over the head dim
    setcfg      ntiles,   NTT                     ; =   4: T keys out
    setcfg      scalar,   RQ_S
    matmul_t.rq scr_lo,   q_head,   kt_head       ; dst, activation, weight:
                                                  ; S8[t][s] int8, the whole [T][T] block.
                                                  ; `scr_lo` is the base — `scr_hi` names
                                                  ; the second half of what this wrote.

    ; ---- P = relu(S8 + mask), in two CHUNK passes ------------------------
    ; The mask is *data*, not control flow: an int8 tensor of 0 (allowed) and
    ; -128 (masked). vecadd widens to int32, the {1,0} requant narrows back and
    ; clips, so a masked entry lands in [-128,-1] — always negative, therefore
    ; always zero after relu — while an allowed entry adds 0 and is unchanged.
    ; That is exactly the model's `relu(scores + -1e9)`, and it is exact for
    ; every score scale rather than a tolerance.
    setcfg      vlen,     CHUNK                   ; two halves, not one 1024 pass
    li          rq_word,  RQ_ID                   ; {m0=1, n=0}: narrow and clip only
    vecadd      acc_i32,  scr_lo,   msk_lo        ; scores + mask       -> int32
    requant     scr_lo,   acc_i32,  rq_word       ; -> int8 in place; masked now <= -1
    vecadd      acc_i32,  scr_hi,   msk_hi        ; ...the other 512
    requant     scr_hi,   acc_i32,  rq_word
    li          rq_word,  RQ_P
    relu        acc_i32,  scr_lo                  ; masked -> exactly 0 -> int32
    requant     att_lo,   acc_i32,  rq_word       ; -> int8 attention weights, into P8
    relu        acc_i32,  scr_hi
    requant     att_hi,   acc_i32,  rq_word

    ; ---- A[:, h*DH : (h+1)*DH] = requant(P_h @ V_h) ---------------------
    ; The output tile lands *directly* in A's column block: `crow` = CROWD makes
    ; the requantized store step D bytes per token row, and the destination base
    ; carries the head's column offset. The previous revision computed A^T and
    ; scattered it back with a `.t` spill through DRAM; that whole detour, and
    ; the DRAM round trip that followed it, existed only because `vecmatmul`
    ; could not write a strided sub-block.
    setcfg      arow,     T                       ; =  32: P rows are T keys
    setcfg      wcol,     TCOL                    ; =   8: a packed V^T row is T trits
    setcfg      crow,     CROWD                   ; = 512: -> int8 rows of D bytes, i.e.
                                                  ; a stride of the *whole* A, not of DH
    setcfg      ktiles,   KTT                     ; =   4: contract over keys
    setcfg      ntiles,   NTDH                    ; =   4: DH columns out
    setcfg      scalar,   RQ_A
    matmul_t.rq a_head,   att_lo,   vt_head       ; dst, activation, weight:
                                                  ; A[t][h*DH + j] int8, written straight
                                                  ; into head h's column block of T1

    ; ---- next head ------------------------------------------------------
    adds        q_head,   q_head,   head_i8       ; Q advances one head_dim of int8 columns
    adds        kt_head,  kt_head,  head_kt       ; ...packed K, one head_dim of trits
    adds        vt_head,  vt_head,  head_vt       ; ...packed V^T, DH whole rows
    adds        a_head,   a_head,   head_i8       ; ...and A's output column block
    adds        loop_i,   loop_i,   one
    cmps        loop_i,   loop_n                  ; loop_n = HEADS here, not NCHUNK
    blt         head_loop

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 5. O = A @ Wo, then the attention residual X <- norm1(X + (O + X))
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; A is already assembled in T1, so the only thing that moves it is the
; verification checkpoint below — the Wo matmul reads it where it lies.

    li          buf_a,    T1                      ; scratchpad side: A
    li          dram_p,   D_A8                    ; DRAM side
    setcfg      len,      B_ACT
    wrmem       buf_a,    dram_p                  ; checkpoint A (adder_kernel.md §7).
                                                  ; Verification only — nothing reads it
                                                  ; back, and A stays in T1.

    li          blk_off,  O_WO
    adds        dram_p,   lay_base, blk_off
    setcfg      len,      B_WD                    ; = D*WCOL = 4096 bytes
    rdmem       wgt_win,  dram_p                  ; Wo over the QKV block in WBUF

    ; Back to the D-deep projection geometry — section 4 left arow/wcol/crow/
    ; ktiles/ntiles set for attention, and config is sticky, so all five have
    ; to be restored even though three of them are being set to what they held
    ; before the head loop.
    setcfg      arow,     AROW                    ; = 128
    setcfg      wcol,     WCOL                    ; =  32
    setcfg      ktiles,   KTD                     ; =  16
    setcfg      ntiles,   NTD                     ; =  16
    setcfg      crow,     CROWD                   ; = 512
    li          buf_a,    T1                      ; activation: A
    li          buf_b,    T2                      ; destination: O
    setcfg      scalar,   RQ_O
    matmul_t.rq buf_b,    buf_a,    wgt_win       ; O = requant(A @ Wo)   [T][D] int8

    ; The attention residual is `2X + O`, not `X + O`: MultiHeadAttention
    ; returns `O + X` and Transformer adds `X` again. `vecadd` takes two int8
    ; operands, so that is two passes with a narrow between them.
    ;
    ; XO = requant(X + O), written *over* O. Both operands are int8 at the same
    ; scale (that is RQ_O's job) and RQ_XO is {1,0}, so XO stays on X's scale and
    ; is a legal operand for the second add. Overwriting O is safe because the
    ; vecadd has already consumed the whole chunk — dispatches are atomic.
    setcfg      vlen,     CHUNK
    li          rq_word,  RQ_XO                   ; {1,0}: stay on X's scale
    li          buf_a,    X                       ; src0: the residual stream
    li          buf_b,    T2                      ; src1 *and* dst: O, then XO
    li          loop_i,   0
    li          loop_n,   NCHUNK
xo_add_loop:
    vecadd      acc_i32,  buf_a,    buf_b         ; X + O               -> int32
    requant     buf_b,    acc_i32,  rq_word       ; -> int8 XO, over O
    adds        buf_a,    buf_a,    chunk_b
    adds        buf_b,    buf_b,    chunk_b
    adds        loop_i,   loop_i,   one
    cmps        loop_i,   loop_n
    blt         xo_add_loop

    ; X1 = norm1(XO + X) = DyT(...), in place over X. `dyt` is the same narrow
    ; as `requant` with a symmetric clip: RQ_X1's multiplier carries norm1's
    ; alpha and lands the result on 1/127, at which point saturating at +-127 is
    ; exactly hardtanh saturating at +-1. No extra pass — the residual add had
    ; to be narrowed anyway, so the normalization is free.
    li          rq_word,  RQ_X1                   ; carries norm1's alpha
    li          buf_a,    X                       ; src0 *and* dst: X, then X1
    li          buf_b,    T2
    li          loop_i,   0
    li          loop_n,   NCHUNK
norm1_loop:
    vecadd      acc_i32,  buf_a,    buf_b
    dyt         buf_a,    acc_i32,  rq_word
    adds        buf_a,    buf_a,    chunk_b
    adds        buf_b,    buf_b,    chunk_b
    adds        loop_i,   loop_i,   one
    cmps        loop_i,   loop_n
    blt         norm1_loop

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 6. Feed-forward: X <- norm2(X1 + W2(relu(W1(X1))))
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; `x_res` still points at X — section 5's `dyt` wrote X1 over it in place — so
; the feed-forward reads the same register the projections did. The MXU geometry
; from section 5 is still live and correct (F == D), so no `setcfg` here but the
; per-matmul `scalar`.

    li          blk_off,  O_W1
    adds        dram_p,   lay_base, blk_off
    setcfg      len,      B_WD
    rdmem       wgt_win,  dram_p                  ; W1 over Wo in WBUF
    li          buf_a,    T1                      ; destination: H
    setcfg      scalar,   RQ_H
    matmul_t.rq buf_a,    x_res,    wgt_win       ; H = requant(X1 @ W1)   [T][F] int8

    setcfg      vlen,     CHUNK
    li          rq_word,  RQ_HR                   ; {1,0} is the natural choice here
    li          buf_a,    T1                      ; source: H
    li          buf_b,    T2                      ; destination: relu(H)
    li          loop_i,   0
    li          loop_n,   NCHUNK
ff_relu_loop:
    relu        acc_i32,  buf_a                   ; int8 -> int32 (two operands, not three)
    requant     buf_b,    acc_i32,  rq_word       ; -> int8
    adds        buf_a,    buf_a,    chunk_b
    adds        buf_b,    buf_b,    chunk_b
    adds        loop_i,   loop_i,   one
    cmps        loop_i,   loop_n
    blt         ff_relu_loop

    li          blk_off,  O_W2
    adds        dram_p,   lay_base, blk_off
    setcfg      len,      B_WD
    rdmem       wgt_win,  dram_p                  ; W2 over W1 in WBUF
    li          buf_a,    T1                      ; destination: F (H is dead now)
    li          buf_b,    T2                      ; activation:  relu(H)
    setcfg      scalar,   RQ_F
    matmul_t.rq buf_a,    buf_b,    wgt_win       ; F = requant(relu(H) @ W2)   [T][D] int8
                                                  ; — note dst and act are swapped relative
                                                  ; to the W1 matmul above

    ; X2 = norm2(X1 + F), again in place and again requiring RQ_F to have landed
    ; F on X1's scale. RQ_X2 carries norm2's alpha and targets 1/127, so this
    ; `dyt` is what makes the *next* layer's input scale known analytically
    ; rather than calibrated — see sharp edge 3 at the top.
    setcfg      vlen,     CHUNK
    li          rq_word,  RQ_X2                   ; carries norm2's alpha
    li          buf_a,    X                       ; src0 *and* dst: X1, then X2
    li          buf_b,    T1                      ; src1: F
    li          loop_i,   0
    li          loop_n,   NCHUNK
norm2_loop:
    vecadd      acc_i32,  buf_a,    buf_b         ; X1 + F              -> int32
    dyt         buf_a,    acc_i32,  rq_word       ; hardtanh -> int8, in place over X
    adds        buf_a,    buf_a,    chunk_b
    adds        buf_b,    buf_b,    chunk_b
    adds        loop_i,   loop_i,   one
    cmps        loop_i,   loop_n
    blt         norm2_loop

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 7. Checkpoint this layer's X, then advance to the next layer block
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; X stays resident in the scratchpad; this spill exists so every layer boundary
; is a comparable intermediate for the integer reference (adder_kernel.md §7),
; not because the data has to move.
    li          blk_off,  O_XOUT
    adds        dram_p,   lay_base, blk_off
    setcfg      len,      B_ACT
    wrmem       x_res,    dram_p                  ; scratch X -> DRAM checkpoint

    li          loop_n,   LSTRIDE                 ; `loop_n` is free: the inner loops are
                                                  ; done, so it doubles as a stride here
    adds        lay_base, lay_base, loop_n        ; next layer's DRAM block
    adds        layer_i,  layer_i,  one
    li          loop_n,   LAYERS
    cmps        layer_i,  loop_n
    blt         layer_loop

; ===========================================================================
; 8. Output head: logits = X @ fc.weight
; ===========================================================================
; `Model.fc` is a TernaryLinear now, so this is an ordinary `matmul_t` against
; the same D-deep geometry the projections use — the array multiplies an int8
; activation by a ternary weight, and X arrives from norm2 at 1/127 as exactly
; that. It was the last `vecmatmul` in the program (int8 X against an int8
; fc.weight, which only the VPU can do); the VPU now runs no matmul at all.
;
; `TernaryLinear.w` is [in, out] = [D][VOCAB] already, which is the column-major
; orientation the array packs a weight in, so nothing is transposed — the same
; property that made `nn.Linear`'s [out, in] convenient for `vecmatmul`, one
; layout over.
;
; **VPAD, not VOCAB.** The array writes a whole COLS-wide tile at a time, so a
; 13-column output is two tiles and the second one writes columns 8..15. With a
; 13-word row stride, columns 13..15 of token t would land on columns 0..2 of
; token t+1 — and the output-tile index is the *outer* loop, so the second tile
; overwrites the first tile's work for every row but the last. Padding the head
; to 16 columns costs 3 zero weight columns (96 bytes) and 384 bytes of logit
; block, and makes the row stride a whole tile. The host stages zero trits above
; VOCAB and reads 13 of every 16 words back.
;
; Spilled as raw int32 with no requant: the host only needs an argmax over 13
; logits, and narrowing first would throw away resolution for nothing.

    li          dram_p,   D_WFC
    setcfg      len,      B_WFC                   ; = VPAD*WCOL = 512 bytes
    rdmem       wgt_win,  dram_p                  ; fc.weight over layer 3's W2

    setcfg      arow,     AROW                    ; = 128: X is [T][D] row-major, as ever
    setcfg      wcol,     WCOL                    ; =  32: a head column is D trits
    setcfg      ktiles,   KTD                     ; =  16: contract the whole model dim
    setcfg      ntiles,   NTV                     ; =   2: VPAD columns out, two tiles
    setcfg      crow,     LROW                    ; =  64: int32 rows of VPAD words. Not
                                                  ; divided by 4 — no `.rq`, so the store
                                                  ; really is int32.
    matmul_t    log_i32,  x_res,    wgt_win       ; no `.rq`: LOG[t][v] stays int32.
                                                  ; `cfg scalar` is whatever layer 3 left
                                                  ; there and is deliberately not read.

    li          dram_p,   D_LOG
    setcfg      len,      B_LOG                   ; = T*VPAD*4 = 2048 bytes
    wrmem       log_i32,  dram_p                  ; the result the host reads back
    halt
